session:pr684Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #525 flagged OntologyDeclarations.individuals (rdf_owl_translator/src/class_expression_parser.rs) as a #[allow(dead_code)] struct field with no issue link, violating CLAUDE.md's dead-code rule. The issue body already contained a thorough investigation concluding the field was genuinely dead rather than just unwired: every assertion-axiom construction site in axiom_parser.rs (ClassAssertion, ObjectPropertyAssertion, DataPropertyAssertion, SameIndividual, DifferentIndividuals) resolves individuals via a stateless try_get_individual() call and never reads decls.individuals; there was no performance case for reusing the map (try_get_individual is already O(1)); and the map would in fact be an incorrect substitute since it only picks up entities with an explicit rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual triple and never produces AnonymousIndividual, so try_get_individual's broader handling would still be needed as a fallback regardless. Given that investigation was already complete and conclusive, this PR did not re-derive it from scratch but did independently re-verify the key claim before acting on it: grepped for `.individuals` usage across rdf_owl_translator/src and confirmed zero read sites for the struct field (only the local `individuals` variable inside build(), which feeds build_annotations, is read). Applied the issue's recommended option 1: deleted the field and its #[allow(dead_code)] attribute from the struct definition and from the struct-literal construction in OntologyDeclarations::build(), keeping the local variable untouched since it is still genuinely used. Pure deletion, no behavior change, so per CLAUDE.md's TDD exception for refactors this was one-pass with the existing test suite as the regression net rather than new tests. Local cargo test --workspace showed one failure (sparql_endpoint::query_timeout, SIGKILL) matching a documented, memory-constrained-machine false alarm unrelated to this change; CI confirmed all real checks (Clippy, Format, Unit & Integration Tests, Tests (release), Docs, Provenance SHACL) green. CI's Security Audit check failed on RUSTSEC-2026-0285 (rustls) — verified via gh issue search that this is a pre-existing, already-tracked, workspace-wide advisory (issues #677 and #678, with a separate worktree already addressing it) rather than something introduced by this PR, which touches no dependencies; noted this on the PR rather than treating it as a blocker." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:684 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr684 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .
